<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE suppressions PUBLIC
    "-//Checkstyle//DTD SuppressionFilter Configuration 1.2//EN"
    "https://checkstyle.org/dtds/suppressions_1_2.dtd">

<!--
  Suppressions are exceptions, and an exception with no reason attached becomes
  permanent. Every entry here must say what is suppressed and why the rule is wrong
  *at that place* rather than wrong in general. "Too noisy" is not a reason to add an
  entry here: it is a reason to change the rule in checkstyle.xml, where the decision
  is visible to everyone instead of hidden behind a path pattern.

  The file is deliberately almost empty. The rules in checkstyle.xml were chosen by
  running them and keeping the ones the code already satisfied, so there was very little
  to excuse: two entries below, each naming one check and one file. Where a rule fired on
  a whole class of correct code instead of one place, it was removed from checkstyle.xml
  with its reasoning rather than hidden here. If you find yourself adding a fourth entry,
  the honest move is usually to fix the code.

  Paths are regular expressions matched against the file name, so [/\\] covers both
  separators and the same file works on Windows and Linux.
-->
<suppressions>

  <!-- Generated and build-output trees. The plugin is pointed at src/main/java, so
       nothing under target/ is scanned today; this entry is what keeps that true for
       a module that later generates sources into target/generated-sources. -->
  <suppress files="[/\\]target[/\\]" checks=".*"/>

  <!-- The JNI declaration block in NativeImageEnhancer is six `private static native`
       methods, each with a one-line javadoc, written as a table:

           /** @return the ABI version the loaded library implements ... */
           private static native int nativeApiVersion();

       SingleLineJavadoc wants each of those expanded to three lines. The reason not to
       is specific to this file: these signatures generate
       native/include/com_parallelimage_nativebridge_NativeImageEnhancer.h, and the way
       anyone verifies the two sides still agree is to put the header next to this block
       and read down. Tripling the height of the block to satisfy a formatting
       preference makes the one review that matters here harder.

       Scoped to the one check and the one file, so a genuinely undocumented method in
       this file is still reported by MissingJavadocType/JavadocStyle. -->
  <suppress files="[/\\]NativeImageEnhancer\.java$" checks="SingleLineJavadoc"/>

  <!-- CliOptions.parse walks argv with an index that doubles as the parse cursor: a flag that
       takes a value reads args[i + 1] and advances past it, six times, identically.
       ModifiedControlVariable is not wrong in general — a loop index changed halfway through a
       body is usually a bug — but in an argv parser advancing the cursor *is* the loop's job, and
       the alternatives are worse. A while loop with a manual `i += takesValue(arg) ? 2 : 1` would
       split "which flags take a value" across the switch and a second list, where the two can
       disagree silently; a `consumed = 2` assignment in each arrow case is the same duplication
       written six times. The loop carries a comment explaining the increment, which is the part a
       reader actually needs.

       Scoped to the one check and the one file: a genuinely accidental index mutation anywhere
       else, including elsewhere in pip-app, is still reported. -->
  <suppress files="[/\\]CliOptions\.java$" checks="ModifiedControlVariable"/>

  <!-- Also honours @SuppressWarnings("checkstyle:RuleName") at the point of use, via
       the SuppressWarningsFilter in checkstyle.xml. Prefer that: an annotation sits
       next to the code it excuses and disappears when that code does, whereas an
       entry in this file outlives the line it was written for. -->

</suppressions>
